The next question makes the answer more serious. The host asks why Mitt did not take the corporate route. Mitt answers first with necessity: people depended on him, and he felt he had no choice. But then he warns against turning entrepreneurship into a romance. In the interview, he claims: $\begin{aligned}
\Pr(\hbox{company breaks even or loses money}) &\approx 0.86,\\
\Pr(R_{\mathrm{company}}>\$10{,}000{,}000) &\approx 0.005.\end{aligned}$ These are interview claims, not externally verified statistics. Their function is to discipline the decision. Ownership alone does not make the owner wealthy.

